.TH apt-offline 8 "February 08, 2025" "version 1.8.6" "USER COMMANDS"
.SH NAME
apt-offline \- Offline APT Package manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B apt-offline
[ARGUMENTS] [OPTIONS]

.SH DESCRIPTION
apt-offline brings
offline package management functionality to Debian based system.  It can be
used to download packages and its dependencies to be installed later on (or
required to update) a disconnected machine.  Packages can be downloaded from a
different connected machine.

.PP
It can also fetch bug reports for the packages that are downloaded.
.PP
Unless the \-h/\-v or \-\-help/\-\-version options are given, one of the
.B get, set, install
or
.B remote
commands must be provided.

.SS set FILENAME [-h] [--install-packages PKG...PKG_N] [--install-src-packages PKG...PKG_N] --src-build-dep [--release release_name] [--update] [--upgrade] [--upgrade-type upgrade]

.PP
.B set
generates a signature file with the data required to install on or update the
disconnected machine.

.B FILENAME {apt-offline.sig}
is the signature file generated on the machine. This file will contain all the
information necessary for apt.

.TP
\-\-simulate
Developer only. Used for testing.

.IP "\fB\-\-install\-packages [PKG...PKG_N]\fP" 10
Packages that need to be installed

.IP "\fB\-\-install\-src\-packages [PKG...PKG_N]\fP" 10
Source packages that need to be installed

.IP "\fB\-\-src\-build\-dep\fP" 10
Download Build Dependency packages for the source packages requested

.IP "\fB\-\-release release_name\fP" 10
Release target to install packages from

.IP "\fB\-\-apt\-backend apt-get\fP" 10
APT backend to use. One of: apt, apt-get, python-apt

.IP "\fB\-\-generate\-changelog\fP" 10
Generate changelog of the version to be downloaded

.IP "\fB\-\-update\fP" 10
Generate APT Database signature for an update. This is the equivalent of using
.B "apt-get update"

.IP "\fB\-\-upgrade\fP" 10
Generate APT Database signature for package upgrade. This is the equivalent of using
.B "apt-get upgrade"

.IP "\fB\-\-upgrade\-type {upgrade_type}\fP" 10
Type of upgrade you would like to perform. Default is
.B "upgrade".
Other valid types are
.B "dist-upgrade" and "dselect-upgrade"

.SS get FILENAME [-h] [--socket-timeout ] [-d / --download-dir] [-s / --cache-dir] [--no-checksum] [-t / --threads ] [--bundle] [--bug-reports] [--simulate] [--proxy-host] [--proxy-port] [--https-cert-file --https-key-file] [--http-basicauth] [--disable-cert-check]

.PP
.B get
downloads APT data as per a signature file.

.B FILENAME {apt-offline.sig}
is the signature file required for details about data to be downloaded. This
file should have been generated by the above
.B set
command.

.IP "\fB\-d, \-\-download\-dir DIR_NAME\fP" 10
Download data to the specified DIR_NAME folder. If no folder name is specified, data is downloaded to a folder in the TEMPDIR path in the format
.B apt-offline-download-$PID

.IP "\fB\-s, \-\-cache\-dir DIR_NAME\fP" 10
Look for data in the cache before downloading it from the internet. If you are on a Debian box, you would want to specify
.I /var/cache/apt/archives
here. If the data is not available in the cache, the downloaded data is also copied to the cache.

.IP "\fB\-\-no\-checksum\fP" 10
Enabling this option will bypass the checksum verification of each downloaded file thus losing integrity of the package. Usage of this option is highly discouraged

.IP "\fB\-t, \-\-threads NUM_OF_THREADS\fP" 10
Number of threads to spawn for downloads. Default is 1. Using too many threads can overload the servers, hence it is advisable to keep the number low

.IP "\fB\-\-bundle FILENAME\fP" 10
Create an archive file FILENAME. The file is archived in zip format

.IP "\fB\-\-bug\-reports\fP" 10
Download bug reports for packages that are being downloaded. Currently only
the Debian BTS is supported.

.IP "\fB\-\-simulate\fP" 10
Print the resolved URI list and do not download. Each line on standard
output is
.B URI <quoted-url> <filename>
where both fields are shell-quoted. The filename is the archive name
.B install
will look up, not a path on this machine. The URL is the one apt already
named; compression fallbacks are not probed. Nothing is written: no
download directory, no bundle file, no cache copy, no bug report.
.B --download-dir
and
.B --bundle
are not required with this option. A note is written to standard error.
This is distinct from
.B set --simulate
and
.B install --simulate,
which are developer dry-runs.

.IP "\fB\-\-proxy\-host\fP" 10
Specify the Proxy Host to be used.

.IP "\fB\-\-proxy\-port\fP" 10
Specify the Proxy port number to be used.

.IP "\fB\-\-https\-cert\-file FILENAME\fP" 10
The Certificate-file to use if https-client-authentication is required on one or multiple hosts.

.IP "\fB\-\-https\-key\-file FILENAME\fP" 10
The Certificate-key-file to use if https-client-authentication is required on one or multiple hosts.

.IP "\fB\-\-disable-cert-check\fP" 10
Disable the check of https certificates on servers

.IP "\fB\-\-http\-basicauth URL\fP" 10
A username/password encoded in the URL. Note\: Passwords with special characters should be percent encoded as per RFC-3986 section 2.1

.SS install FILE/FOLDER [-h] [--skip-bug-reports ] [--install-src-path PATH] [--allow-unauthenticated]

.PP
.B install
installs APT data to the APT package database and updates it.

.B FILE {archive_bundle_file}
Install data from the archive (bundle) file.

.B FOLDER {/folder/path}
Install data from the folder path.

Either FILE or FOLDER argument can be provided to the
.B install
command.

.TP
\-\-simulate
Developer only. Used for testing.

.IP "\fB\-\-skip\-bug\-reports\fP" 10
Skip listing of downloaded bug reports, if any.

.IP "\fB\-\-skip\-changelog\fP" 10
Skip display of changelog, if any.

.IP "\fB\-\-allow\-unauthenticated\fP" 10
Don't verify GPG signatures for the data to be installed to APT. Usage of this option is highly discouraged.

.IP "\fB\-\-strict\-deb\-check\fP" 10
With this option enabled, apt-offline delegate's .deb package checksum validation to apt. While the .debs are
already available, they are stored in the temporary apt cache, where apt validates its checksum, before considering
it for further processing.
Note: This does have the caveat that apt may need network availability even though it doesn't download anything over the network. But it does invoke the download routines and realizes that the payload is already available. It then further proceeds with checksum validation

The default behavior is to
.B not
do strict checksum validation for .deb files. Instead, apt-offline copies the .deb files to apt's download location. apt still does size validation of the available .deb files and discards them in case there is a mismatch.

.IP "\fB\-\-install\-src\-path PATH\fP" 10
Path to filesystem where we want the source packages to be installed to. Default will be a folder in your TEMPDIR.

.SS remote [SSH_HOST ...| --hosts-list FILE] [-h] [--update] [--upgrade] [--dist-upgrade] [--install-packages PKG...] [--reboot] [--work-dir DIR | --temp] [--force] [--cache-metadata] [--no-pkg-cache] [--keep-latest [N]] [--clean-remote] [--clean-cache] [--clean-pkg-cache] [--clean-metadata-cache] [--status] [--fetch | --download | --finish-install]

.PP
.B remote
automates the full set/get/install pipeline against a machine reachable over SSH,
eliminating the need for manual multi-step scripting.
The local machine must have internet connectivity; the remote machine need only
have
.B apt-offline
installed.

.B SSH_HOST
is one or more target machines, each specified as an SSH alias from
.I ~/.ssh/config
or as
.I [user@]hostname
as accepted by
.BR ssh (1).
Multiple hosts are separated by whitespace.
Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-hosts\-list .

.IP "\fB\-\-hosts\-list FILE\fP" 10
Text file containing one SSH host per line.
Lines starting with
.B #
and blank lines are ignored.
Inline comments (text following a
.B #
on the same line as a host) are also stripped.
Applies the requested operation to all listed hosts in sequence,
reporting a per-host summary at the end.
Mutually exclusive with
.BR SSH_HOST .
If neither
.B SSH_HOST
nor
.B \-\-hosts\-list
is given and
.B \-\-work\-dir
is set, a file named
.B hosts.list
in the work directory is used automatically.

.IP "\fB\-\-update\fP" 10
Generate and apply an APT database update on the remote machine.

.IP "\fB\-\-upgrade\fP" 10
Download and apply upgradable packages on the remote machine.

.IP "\fB\-\-dist\-upgrade\fP" 10
Perform a full dist-upgrade on the remote machine.

.IP "\fB\-\-install\-packages [PKG...PKG_N]\fP" 10
Packages to install on the remote machine.

.PP
Before any operation,
.B remote
verifies that
.B apt-offline
is installed on the remote host (via
.BR "which apt-offline" ).
If it is missing, the command fails immediately with an install hint.
.PP
Remote commands that require root privileges are automatically prefixed with
.B sudo
unless the connecting user is already root (detected by checking
.B id -u
on the remote).
The remote user must have passwordless sudo configured for the commands
apt-offline needs (apt-offline, apt-get, mkdir, reboot).
If sudo requires a password, the command fails early with the required
sudoers entry.
No flag is needed.

.IP "\fB\-\-reboot\fP" 10
Reboot the remote machine after a successful operation.
Can also be used standalone (without any operation flag) to reboot one
or more hosts without performing any other operation.
Cannot be combined with
.B \-\-fetch
or
.BR \-\-download .
.B Warning:
when used with
.B \-\-hosts\-list
and some hosts act as SSH jumphosts for others in the list, exercise
caution with ordering.
The tool processes hosts sequentially and has no knowledge of jump
topology.
If a jumphost is rebooted and the very next host in the list routes
through it, the SSH tunnel may drop mid-operation (mid-transfer or
mid
.BR "apt-get upgrade" )
leaving the remote in an unknown state \(em the remote process may
continue running unattended while the tool reports an error.
Place all hosts that route through a jumphost
.B before
that jumphost in the list so it is rebooted last; or avoid
.B \-\-reboot
with mixed topologies altogether and reboot manually in dependency
order after the batch completes.

.IP "\fB\-\-work\-dir DIR\fP" 10
Local directory under which per-host working directories are created to store
signature and bundle files.
When omitted, files are kept under
.B ~/.cache/apt-offline/SSH_HOST/
and persist across reboots.
Each run creates a uniquely timestamped operation subdirectory of the form
.B <timestamp>-<tag>/
(where tag is one of
.BR upd ,
.BR upg ,
.BR dup ,
.BR ipk )
containing
.BR apt-offline.sig ,
.B bundle.zip
and
.BR state.json ,
making parallel execution against multiple hosts and bundle reuse both safe.
The shared package and metadata caches are kept directly under this directory as
.B pkg-cache/
and
.BR metadata-cache/ .
Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-temp .

.IP "\fB\-\-temp\fP" 10
Use
.B /tmp/apt-offline/SSH_HOST/
as the working directory instead of the default cache location.
Files placed under
.I /tmp
are lost on reboot.
Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-work\-dir .

.IP "\fB\-\-keep\-latest [N]\fP" 10
Remove all but the
.I N
most recent timestamped operation directories from the
local per-host working directory.
.I N
defaults to 1 when the flag is given without a value.
Must be used without any phase flag; when also omitting operation flags it
performs a standalone local cleanup with no SSH connection.
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-fetch ,
.B \-\-download
or
.BR \-\-finish\-install .

.IP "\fB\-\-clean\-remote\fP" 10
Remove the
.B ~/.cache/apt-offline
directory on the remote host.
Can be combined with
.B \-\-keep\-latest
to clean both sides in one command.
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-fetch ,
.B \-\-download
or
.BR \-\-finish\-install .

.IP "\fB\-\-force\fP" 10
Overwrite an existing
.B bundle.zip
instead of failing.
Only valid with
.B \-\-download
or the full pipeline; rejected with
.B \-\-fetch
or
.BR \-\-finish\-install .

.IP "\fB\-\-cache\-metadata\fP" 10
Cache metadata files (Release, Packages, Translation, etc.) under
.B <work\-dir>/metadata\-cache/
keyed by a SHA256 of the URL, so identical files are not re-downloaded for
each host in a batch run.
Off by default because cached metadata may be stale across separate runs.
404 responses are also cached (negative cache) to avoid retrying all
compression formats for every host.

.IP "\fB\-\-no\-pkg\-cache\fP" 10
Disable the local package cache.
By default
.B .deb
files are cached under
.B <work\-dir>/pkg\-cache/
and reused across hosts and runs (verified by checksum where available).

.IP "\fB\-\-clean\-cache\fP" 10
Remove both
.B pkg\-cache/
and
.B metadata\-cache/
under the local work directory.
These caches are shared across all hosts and are removed once regardless
of how many hosts are specified.
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-fetch ,
.B \-\-download
or
.BR \-\-finish\-install .

.IP "\fB\-\-clean\-pkg\-cache\fP" 10
Remove only the
.B pkg\-cache/
directory under the local work directory.
Operates at the work-dir level, not per-host.
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-fetch ,
.B \-\-download
or
.BR \-\-finish\-install .

.IP "\fB\-\-clean\-metadata\-cache\fP" 10
Remove only the
.B metadata\-cache/
directory under the local work directory.
Operates at the work-dir level, not per-host.
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-fetch ,
.B \-\-download
or
.BR \-\-finish\-install .

.IP "\fB\-\-status\fP" 10
Display the pipeline status of local runs for the given host(s).
For each timestamped operation directory shows the date, operation type,
and whether the fetch, download and install phases have completed.
Download completion is confirmed via a flag in
.B state.json
written only when the fetcher exits without errors, not merely by
the presence of a
.B bundle.zip
file.
Reads only local state; no SSH connection is made.

.PP
In a batch run, hosts are reported as
.B OK
(succeeded),
.B WARN
(skipped — already up to date or no local state for the requested phase), or
.B ERROR
(failed).
Only actual errors produce a non-zero exit code; skipped hosts do not.

.PP
When none of the phase flags below is given,
.B remote
runs all three phases back-to-back in a single invocation.
This is the simplest mode and requires the local machine to have internet
connectivity throughout the operation.
Use the individual phase flags only when the local machine must go offline
between the signature fetch and the package download
(e.g. to carry files on removable media to an air-gapped network).

.IP "\fB\-\-fetch\fP" 10
Phase 1: connect to the remote, generate the signature file and fetch it
locally. Creates a timestamped operation subdirectory containing the
signature and a state file so subsequent phases know which operation was
originally requested.
Operation flags
.RB ( \-\-update ,
.BR \-\-upgrade ,
.BR \-\-dist\-upgrade ,
.BR \-\-install\-packages )
are required here and cannot be used with
.B \-\-download
or
.BR \-\-finish\-install ,
which read the operation from the saved state.

.IP "\fB\-\-download\fP" 10
Phase 2: run
.B apt-offline get
locally to download packages and produce the bundle, using the signature
from the latest
.B \-\-fetch
run. Intended for use on a good internet connection after
.B \-\-fetch
has completed.
If the remote is already up to date the signature will be empty and the
command exits cleanly with a "nothing to do" message.
Use
.B \-\-force
to overwrite an existing bundle from a previous run.
If any files fail to download, the failure summary includes the error
reason and distinguishes transient network failures (which may succeed
on retry) from permanent ones (404).

.IP "\fB\-\-finish\-install\fP" 10
Phase 3: push the bundle produced by
.B \-\-download
to the remote, install it with
.B apt-offline install
and run the corresponding
.B apt-get
operation non-interactively, preserving locally modified configuration
files on conflict.
The operation type is read from the state file written by
.BR \-\-fetch .
Fails immediately if the download phase did not complete successfully
(i.e. the
.B downloaded
flag in
.B state.json
is not set), preventing an install attempt with a potentially
incomplete bundle.
Cannot be combined with operation flags.

.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
\-h, \-\-help
Show help message

.TP
\-\-verbose
Run in verbose mode

.TP
\-v, \-\-version
Display the version of the program


.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B NOTE: argument/option handling
apt\-offline relies on argparse for argument/option parsing. To explicitly instruct apt\-offline about an argument, you can pass it with the \-\- delimiter.

.B Ex. apt\-offline set \-\-update \-\-upgrade \-\-install\-packages wm2 \-\- foo.sig

By specifying the
.B \-\-
delimiter, we instruct apt\-offline that foo.sig is an argument to the
.B apt\-offline
command and not to the
.B \-\-install\-packages
option.

Otherwise, you could also use it positionally next to the set command

.B Ex. apt\-offline set foo.sig \-\-update \-\-upgrade \-\-install\-packages wm2

.TP
.B apt-offline set FILENAME
This command will generate a signature file FILENAME for APT Package Database.
To generate only the signature for updates, use the \-\-update option.  To
generate only the signature for package upgrades, use the \-\-upgrade option.

.TP
.B apt-offline get FILENAME
This command will fetch the data required for APT Package Database as per the
signature file FILENAME generated by
.B apt-offline get.
To download bug reports also use the \-\-bug\-reports option.  Currently supported bug tracker is Debian BTS only.
By default, if neither of \-d or \-\-bundle options are specified, apt-offline downloads data into a folder inside the TEMPDIR environment folder in the format apt\-offline\-downloads\-PID, where PID is the PID of the running apt\-offline process. Example on a Linux machine would be something like: /tmp/apt-offline-downloads-23242/

.TP
.B apt-offline install FILE|FOLDER
This command will sync the data downloaded by
.B apt-offline get
to the APT Package Database and update it.
Depending on where the data was downloaded to or packed into, either the
absolute FOLDER path or the archive FILE path can be specified.

.B NOTE1:
On a freshly installed box, that was installed without the network, the package database is null. In that case, you first need to run
.B apt-offline
with just the
.B \-\-update
option to ensure you have a meaningful package database

.B Example: apt-offline set set.uris \-\-update

.B NOTE2:
On a fresh setup installed through CD/DVD, the default APT setting lists only the install media URLs. In such case, you need to add the default APT network repositories to the list. For example, for a fresh (DVD) installed Debian box, add the relevant repository to

.I /etc/apt/sources.list.d/apt-offline.list or /etc/apt/sources.list

deb https://deb.debian.org/debian stable main contrib

.B (For Debian Stable)

deb https://deb.debian.org/debian unstable main contrib

.B (For Debian Unstable/Sid)

deb https://deb.debian.org/debian stretch main contrib

.B (For Debian Stretch)

deb http://security.debian.org stable/updates main contrib

.B (Security Updates for Debian Stable)

deb http://security.debian.org testing-security/updates main contrib

.B (Security Updates for Debian Testing)

.TP
.B apt-offline remote SSH_HOST [SSH_HOST ...]
This command automates the entire set/get/install workflow over SSH.
The local machine downloads the required data and pushes it to the remote.
At least one of \-\-update, \-\-upgrade, \-\-dist\-upgrade or \-\-install\-packages must be specified.
Multiple hosts can be given as whitespace-separated positional arguments,
or via a file with \-\-hosts\-list.

.B One-shot (local machine stays online throughout):

.B apt\-offline remote myserver \-\-update \-\-upgrade

.B One-shot against multiple hosts with caching enabled:

.B apt\-offline remote host1 host2 host3 \-\-update \-\-upgrade \-\-cache\-metadata

.B Split workflow (local machine goes offline between phases):

.B apt\-offline remote myserver \-\-update \-\-upgrade \-\-fetch

(Phase 1: generate and retrieve the signature. Run while connected to the remote.)

.B apt\-offline remote myserver \-\-download

(Phase 2: download packages locally. Run when internet is available.)

.B apt\-offline remote myserver \-\-finish\-install

(Phase 3: push the bundle to the remote and complete installation.)

.TP
Sequence 1: The following set of commands, when run in sequence, will update a disconnected machine.

.B apt-offline set update.sig \-\-update

(Generate the required data needed to update the APT database. Should be run on the disconnected machine)

.B apt-offline get update.sig \-\-bundle update.zip

(Download the required data needed to update the APT database. Should be run on a machine with internet connectivity)

.B apt-offline install update.zip

(Installs the data needed to update the APT database. Should be run on the disconnected machine)

.TP
Sequence 2: With successful completion of Sequence 1, the APT database on the disconnected machine will be up\-to\-date. Now, the following set of commands, when run in sequence, will upgrade a disconnected machine.

.B apt-offline set upgrade.sig \-\-upgrade

(Generate the required data needed to upgrade the upgradable packages. Should be run on the disconnected machine)

.B apt-offline get upgrade.sig \-\-bundle upgrade.zip

(Download the required data needed to upgrade the upgradable packages. Should be run on a machine with internet connectivity)

.B apt-offline install upgrade.zip

(Installs the data needed to upgrade the upgradable packages. Should be run on the disconnected machine)

.TP
After successful completion of
.B Sequence 1
and
.B Sequence 2
in order, further running
.B apt-get upgrade
will result in 0 bytes of additional download.

